# Phase 1: Authentication & Authorization — FindLocal

## Overview

This phase adds secure, role-based authentication to the existing Express/MongoDB backend. The approach is deliberate and minimal: standard JWT in `HttpOnly` cookies, bcrypt for passwords, and a clean middleware chain for protecting routes. No Redis, no OAuth, no microservices — just a well-structured pattern a single MERN developer can maintain and explain in an interview.

---

## 1. User Model / Schema

A single `User` collection serves all three roles. Role is stored as a field, not as separate collections. This keeps queries simple and avoids complex joins.

### Fields

| Field | Type | Notes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `name` | String | Required, trimmed |
| `email` | String | Required, unique, lowercased, indexed |
| `password` | String | Required, hashed — never returned in responses |
| `role` | String (enum) | `'customer'` \| `'seller'` \| `'admin'`. Default: `'customer'` |
| `isVerified` | Boolean | For sellers: admin must approve. Default: `false` for sellers, `true` for customers/admin |
| `isActive` | Boolean | Soft-ban flag. Default: `true` |
| `createdAt` / `updatedAt` | Date | Via Mongoose `timestamps: true` |

> [!NOTE]
> **Why a single User collection?** Three separate collections (Customer, Seller, Admin) would require three auth flows, three middleware checks, and duplicate code. A single `User` model with a `role` field is the MERN standard pattern. You'll explain this in interviews as "single-table role discrimination" or "polymorphic users."

### Mongoose Schema Options
- `timestamps: true` — auto-manages `createdAt` and `updatedAt`.
- `select: false` on the `password` field — password is **excluded from all queries by default**. You must explicitly request it only when needed (e.g., during login).

