## 16. Common Beginner Mistakes to Avoid

| # | Mistake | Correct Approach |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| 1 | Storing JWT in `localStorage` | Use `HttpOnly` cookies |
| 2 | Returning `password` in API responses | Use `select: false` + never include in `.toJSON()` |
| 3 | Comparing ObjectIds with `===` | Always use `.toString()` on both sides |
| 4 | Hashing password in the controller | Hash in the Mongoose pre-save hook |
| 5 | Re-hashing password on non-password updates | Check `if (this.isModified('password'))` in the hook |
| 6 | Using `role: 'admin'` in the register endpoint | Block admin self-registration explicitly |
| 7 | Giving different 401 messages for "user not found" vs "wrong password" | Same message always — prevents user enumeration |
| 8 | Trusting JWT payload without DB re-fetch | Always re-fetch user in `protect` to catch bans/deletions |
| 9 | Skipping `await` on async Mongoose calls | Every `.find()`, `.save()`, `.create()` is async |
| 10 | Forgetting `cookie-parser` in `server.js` | `req.cookies` will be `undefined` without it |
| 11 | Hardcoding `JWT_SECRET` in source code | Always from `process.env.JWT_SECRET` |
| 12 | Not setting `secure: true` in production cookies | Cookies sent over HTTP in production are interceptable |
| 13 | Merging `protect` and `authorize` into one middleware | Keep identity (protect) and permission (authorize) separate |
| 14 | Checking ownership in middleware instead of controller | Ownership depends on resource type; do it in the controller |
| 15 | Using `delete user.password` on a Mongoose doc | Use `select: false` or `.toObject()` + `delete`; Mongoose docs don't behave like plain objects |

